---
import Sheet from "../layouts/Sheet.astro";
import Demo from "../components/Demo.astro";
import SnippetBlock from "../components/SnippetBlock.astro";
import { standaloneSnippets } from "../data/standalone-snippets.ts";
---

<Sheet
  title="watershed — MV-register revision slate"
  description="Race two revisions, keep both, then resolve what you've read. A live multi-value register running watershed's compiled Gleam kernel."
>
  <header class="mv-hero">
    <p class="annot"><a href="/structures/maps">Maps &amp; cells</a> / Revision slate</p>
    <h1>Converged.<br />Still <em>two answers.</em></h1>
    <p>
      A last-write-wins cell would discard one revision. This one keeps the
      disagreement. Race "raise crest" against "arm pump": all three clients
      reach the same two alternatives. Nothing is stuck in transit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once you've read both, resolve with "raise crest + arm pump".
      That's an ordinary write, not a special conflict API. An unseen third
      revision would survive it.
    </p>
  </header>
  <main>
    <Demo views={["mv-register"]} showPicker={false} heading="One slate, three field crews">
      <div slot="intro">
        <p>
          Confirmed alternatives stay in ink; your pending revision appears
          in magenta. Cut Client B's link, write on both sides, then restore it.
          Agreement means every crew sees the same alternatives, not necessarily
          a single answer.
        </p>
        <p>
          Re-deliver keeps an earlier delta even after resolution. The old
          revision stays retired. Reset tears off a fresh slate and discards
          delayed work from the previous one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Demo>
    <section class="mv-notes">
      <h2>A list isn't a queue</h2>
      <p>
        <code>mv_register_values</code> returns a result containing a sorted
        <code>List(String)</code>. The order is for display, not priority.
        A fresh register reads <code>[]</code>; writing an empty string reads
        <code>[""]</code>. There's no delete hidden in that spelling.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two concurrent writes of the same text still produce two entries.
        Don't deduplicate them and mistake that for causal agreement. A fresh
        write after observing both replaces both, even if its text is unchanged.
        The clock advances, though no visible-value event need fire.
      </p>
      <h2>One typed field on the sequenced stream</h2>
      <p>
        The JavaScript facade's ensure callback returns an <code>MvRegister</code>
        handle. Ensure waits for synchronization before looking up or creating
        the field; it doesn't make the next user write wait for acknowledgement.
        The read below includes that optimistic write. On the BEAM,
        <code>watershed_beam</code> has the corresponding typed API with blocking
        ensures and subject subscriptions.
      </p>
      <SnippetBlock snippet={standaloneSnippets["mv-register-sequenced"]} />
      <h2>Or let the peers exchange revisions</h2>
      <p>
        A CRDT document can use this register as its root. Connect with this
        configuration, then use the document delivered by a successful readiness
        callback. If you're filling a missing default rather than handling a
        user edit, wait for readiness and inspect the synchronized value first:
        an early seed can become a concurrent alternative.
      </p>
      <SnippetBlock snippet={standaloneSnippets["mv-register-p2p"]} />
      <h2>The merge example is a regression, too</h2>
      <p>
        This is the kernel test the suite runs, not a second merge implementation
        written for the page. A and B author independently, observe each other's
        alternatives, then A writes a combined revision. An unseen C would not
        be superseded: resolution covers only the history the writer observed.
      </p>
      <SnippetBlock snippet={standaloneSnippets["mv-register-concurrent-resolution"]} />
      <p>
        Compare the <a href="/structures/maps#mv-register">maps field sheet</a>
        with <a href="/structures/coordination#registers">RegisterCollection</a>,
        which maintains atomic and last-write-wins views using server sequence
        numbers, and <a href="/structures/coordination#pact">PactMap</a>, which
        waits for a frozen quorum's protocol acknowledgements. None of those
        acknowledgements means a person approved a revision. Here, the
        application decides when and how to offer a resolving write.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Sheet>

<script>
  import { initReveals } from "../scripts/motion.js";
  initReveals();
</script>

<style>
  .mv-hero {
    padding: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 6rem) var(--content-pad);
    max-width: 70rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  .mv-hero p {
    max-width: 65ch;
  }
  .mv-notes {
    max-width: 70rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 3rem var(--content-pad);
  }
  .mv-notes p {
    max-width: 70ch;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .mv-notes h2 {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }
</style>
